Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API:

---

**Program.cs** (stdio transport):

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("WeatherApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weather.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

**WeatherTools.cs** (tool class wrapping a REST API):

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        [Description("Units: 'metric' or 'imperial'")] string units = "metric",
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetAsync(
            $"/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&units={units}",
            cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current temperature for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetTemperature(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var data = await client.GetFromJsonAsync<TempResponse>(
            $"/temperature?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}",
            cancellationToken);
        return $"{data?.Temp}°C in {city}";
    }

    private record TempResponse(double Temp, string Unit);
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | The SDK creates tool class instances via DI. Register services in `Program.cs` and accept them as constructor params (shown above with primary constructor syntax). |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services can appear directly as method parameters. The SDK resolves them — they **never** appear in the tool's JSON schema exposed to LLMs. |
| **Auto-injected types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and any DI-registered service are injected automatically. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM. |

**Key rule:** The SDK distinguishes "schema parameters" (exposed to LLM, require `[Description]`) from "injected parameters" (resolved from DI or framework). This means you can freely mix user-facing inputs with internal services in the same method signature.

---

To scaffold this project from scratch:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build
```
